{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Oregon's pioneering approach to decriminalizing drug possession is ending this Sunday, replaced by legislation that reinstates criminal penalties for holding small quantities of hard substances. This new statute, HB4002, allows individuals caught with items like fentanyl or meth to choose between facing charges or entering a treatment track. The goal is to facilitate police action against public drug use while imposing stricter consequences for sales near parks and other sensitive areas.\n\n***\n\nUnder the revised rules, possessing drugs for personal use becomes a misdemeanor carrying a potential six-month jail sentence. While the law pushes counties to establish diversion programs that link individuals to addiction and mental health support, it does not force them to do so. Proponents argue this flexibility lets localities tailor solutions to their specific budgets, whereas critics fear it will result in an inconsistent and unfair system across the region.\n\n***\n\nPortland Police Chief Bob Day noted that the criteria for avoiding jail are quite strict, requiring individuals to have no other charges, warrants, or history of violence, and to be medically stable. If an officer encounters someone who might qualify, they must contact a dispatch line immediately. A mobile health team then has a thirty-minute window to reach the individual; if they fail to arrive in time, the person will be taken into custody, as officers cannot wait indefinitely.\n\n***\n\nThis shift in policy reflects a broader debate on how a **country** should handle addiction, though the specific **age** of the laws does not change the urgency of the crisis. Some observers suggest that without proper support, people might **eat** from trash or engage in desperate **shopping** for substances rather than seeking help. Ultimately, the **tongue** of the law speaks of accountability, yet the effectiveness relies entirely on the rapid response of health services to prevent incarceration.\n\nP.P.S This summary captures the essential elements of the legislative change described in the source text.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -8.106264e-06}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.05084317}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.28708962}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -1.7857208}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -2.1100266e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.13180469}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "izing", "logprob": -2.6703237e-05}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.1553201}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.06654439}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.032884832}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.3229635}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.029021835}, {"token": " Sunday", "logprob": -0.0011748418}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1827597}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.16789734}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " legislation", "logprob": -0.44021004}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00010002159}, {"token": " reinst", "logprob": -0.14931725}, {"token": "ates", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " criminal", "logprob": -0.00066558813}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.009622649}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " holding", "logprob": -0.007050395}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -7.271793e-06}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.18270439}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.012796449}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.00083564856}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.00276351}, {"token": " This", "logprob": -0.45929956}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.023331407}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.17841327}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.02872594}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -0.29610196}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -7.629424e-06}, {"token": "0", "lo